00:05:10.625 --> 00:05:16.454
gravity. Making starships return fuel on Mars from an engineering standpoint is a closed

00:05:16.454 --> 00:05:21.974
chemical loop that turns local ice and air into methane and oxygen. The saboteer reaction

00:05:21.974 --> 00:05:26.894
feeds carbon dioxide from the atmosphere with hydrogen cracked out of water ice, and

00:05:26.894 --> 00:05:31.534
it runs at 300-400 degrees Celsius over a nickel or ruthenium catalyst.

00:05:32.194 --> 00:05:37.254
Methane and water come out, you then electrolyze that water to recover the hydrogen, and to make

00:05:37.254 --> 00:05:41.294
the oxygen demanded by a 3.6-1 oxidizer to fuel mix.

00:05:41.994 --> 00:05:46.594
A membrane sitting on the reactor pulls water vapor out as it forms, so the reaction

00:05:46.594 --> 00:05:51.434
keeps driving forward even when solar power blinks, and the same water never leaves the

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:51.614
loop.

00:05:52.194 --> 00:05:57.354
Once the ice and the air are in reach, the feedstock is effectively free, and costs collapses

00:05:57.354 --> 00:06:02.594
from an Earth launch price of roughly $1,000 per kilogram down to the electricity for

00:06:02.594 --> 00:06:04.214
electrolysis and liquid faction.

00:06:04.214 --> 00:06:11.714
A full Starship stack still wants roughly 1,150 tons of methane per flight, about 22% of

00:06:11.714 --> 00:06:12.514
the propellant load.

00:06:13.074 --> 00:06:18.774
A back-of-envelope figure for the oxygen that rides with it is about 4,100 tons, which

00:06:18.774 --> 00:06:22.654
puts the whole methalox load on the order of 5,300 tons.

00:06:23.334 --> 00:06:29.714
Lofting that methane from Earth at $1,000 a kilogram would be on the order of 1.15 billion dollars

00:06:29.714 --> 00:06:30.974
in launched mass alone.

00:06:31.494 --> 00:06:33.154
That is the magic wand number.

00:06:33.154 --> 00:06:36.874
Raw Martian air is about 95% carbon dioxide.

00:06:37.474 --> 00:06:42.154
The ice is already on the ground, and the idiot index versus earth-launched methane sits

00:06:42.154 --> 00:06:45.374
in the ballpark of a thousand to one if you count only energy.

00:06:45.994 --> 00:06:49.314
Saboteer needs four hydrogen molecules per methane molecule.

00:06:49.754 --> 00:06:54.174
Then the water you get back returns half of that hydrogen when you split it, so you are

00:06:54.174 --> 00:06:56.074
not hauling the hydrogen in from Earth.

00:06:56.534 --> 00:07:01.774
That loop only yields about two kilograms of oxygen per kilogram of methane, short of the

00:07:01.774 --> 00:07:06.894
mix the engines want, so extra ice still has to be mined and split, on the order of

00:07:06.894 --> 00:07:12.114
2,000 tons of additional water per flight. Electrolizing water takes on the order of

00:07:12.114 --> 00:07:17.154
50 kilowatt hours per kilogram of hydrogen, which means the plant is a power station that

00:07:17.154 --> 00:07:21.534
happens to make rocket fuel, and it has to live with sunlight that dies every night.

00:07:22.114 --> 00:07:26.674
The membrane's job is to keep converting carbon dioxide through those power swings instead

00:07:26.674 --> 00:07:29.374
of stalling in a wet reactor that has hit equilibrium.

00:07:29.374 --> 00:07:34.854
Tests with membrane integration have shown substantial gains in carbon dioxide conversion

00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:38.734
and long-term stability across a wide range of reaction conditions.

00:07:39.374 --> 00:07:43.774
The same water recycling chemistry also lets you make hydrogen when the solar array is

00:07:43.774 --> 00:07:48.554
up and coast when it is not, which is what you want from months of surface operations.

00:07:49.214 --> 00:07:55.154
Net propellant therefore depends only on accessible local carbon dioxide and ice, not on tanks

00:07:55.154 --> 00:07:56.054
launch from Earth.

